Senior official of the Ministry of Water Resources instructs the Svay Rieng Provincial Administration to restore Boeung Batou Lake, Chantrea District according to the technical specifications to store water for use.
(Svay Rieng): On the occasion of inspecting the condition of irrigation system and reservoir in Svay Rieng province, Mr. Chhea Bunrith, Permanent Secretary of State of the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology, instructed the Svay Rieng Provincial Administration to rehabilitate Batu Reservoir in Chantrea District and carry out rehabilitation according to Technical specifications issued by the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology and a progress report.
On that occasion, Mr. Sor Sodany said that the team inspected the condition of irrigation system and reservoir in Svay Rieng province, including:
1: Examining the case of 254 families enjoying the bait in the submerged basin, applying for a title deed in Sangke commune, Bos Morn commune and Pong Teuk commune, Romduol district, Svay Rieng province, which covers an area of 297 hectares, no longer as a reservoir. In addition, the people agreed to keep the 3,495-meter-long Prek Chak land and the 8,349-meter-long dam as public property of the state.
2: Inspecting the location of a private company digging the land of Thlak Kambot reservoir without permission, intending to build a dam to encroach on the reservoir land in Chres commune, Chantrea district, after inspecting and with the coordination of the local authority, the private company has cleared the land.
3. Checking the condition of Mekich canal, the Permanent Secretary of State instructed the Director of the Department of Irrigation and Agriculture and the Deputy Director of the Department of Engineering to examine the possibility of repair and maintenance in 2025.
It should be noted that this Mekich canal is 53 kilometers long and passes through three districts, including Romeas Hek, Romduol and Svay Teap districts in Svay Rieng province. Senior officials of the Ministry of Rural Development instruct officials in provincial departments to maintain existing achievements and build new ones
