What was the focus of the 105-minute dialogue between Chinese and US leaders?
BEIJING (Reuters) - Chinese and US leaders held a 105-minute telephone conversation and talked frankly with each other. Chinese leaders have warned US leaders not to get involved in the Taiwan issue, while US leaders have warned China on a number of issues.
White House officials described the phone call as "honest, open and constructive." This is the first dialogue between the two superpowers since the two leaders' summit in November last year in California, which then re-established military ties between the two countries. Pledges stronger cooperation on stopping the flow of fentanyl from China.
The two leaders discussed Taiwan on the eve of the inauguration of Taiwan's new president, Lai Ching-te, who vowed to defend Taiwan's independence from Chinese rule but wanted to strengthen its independence and independence with China. Other democracies.
Biden still spoke of putting pressure on China over its defense ties with Russia, which is seeking to rebuild its industrial base as Russia moves forward with the war in Ukraine. And Joe Biden called on Beijing to use its influence over North Korea to further curb North Korea's nuclear weapons program.
Xi Jinping complained that the United States has taken more steps to suppress China's economy, trade and technology in recent months, and the list of sanctioned Chinese companies has become more and more popular. However, this is not an exaggeration, but it creates more risk.
