Many schools in the Philippine capital of Manila have suspended classes in the classroom due to the dangerous heat.
MANILA (Reuters) - Dozens of schools in the Manila metropolitan area were suspended on Tuesday due to heat levels, according to education officials in the Philippines. This was released by the AFP news agency on Tuesday, April 2, 2024.
The Philippine Meteorological Agency said the temperature index would reach 42 degrees Celsius in Manila on Tuesday and 43 degrees Celsius on Wednesday. The temperature index was calculated taking into account the temperature and humidity in the air, while the actual maximum temperature forecast for Manila on Tuesday was 35 degrees Celsius.
Elementary and high schools in the Quezon area of Manila have been ordered to close, while schools in other areas have been given the option of distance learning. Some schools have also reduced their study hours to avoid the hottest Tuesday.
It should be noted that the temperature index between 42-51 degrees Celsius can cause other symptoms and if left under extreme heat for a long time, it can lead to fainting because the body can no longer control the temperature. France wants a solution. UN Security Council to monitor Gaza ceasefire
(UN) - France on Monday proposed a draft resolution by the United Nations Security Council (UN) tasked with monitoring the ceasefire in Gaza and another proposal to help the Palestinian Authority begin. Its duties. This is according to the release by Reuters on Tuesday, April 2, 2024.
Israel's ally, the United States, did not veto last month's vote to allow the council to demand an immediate ceasefire for the Muslim fasting month, which ends next week. Including the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages. But it was not followed by the warring parties.
