Cambodia and Asia-Pacific countries share knowledge and jointly address climate change challenges
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia, in collaboration with the Climate Investment Fund and the Asian Development Bank, has organized the "Asia-Pacific Knowledge Exchange Program" to share knowledge, experiences, lessons and ideas to address climate change challenges in Cambodia. The Asia-Pacific region is becoming a complex issue in the new era to achieve climate change resilience.
Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Economy and Finance, stated that climate change is a global issue that requires all of us to take action responsibly and with high commitment. To reduce vulnerability by building resilience and better adaptation.
He added that climate change is not only causing global warming, but also causing more and more serious natural disasters. Asia, which accounts for about 60% of the world's population, is among the most vulnerable to climate change.
In fact, in 2022, in Asia, natural disasters destroyed the homes of more than 50 million people and caused economic losses of up to 80 billion US dollars. This underscores the severity and vulnerability of climate change.
The Climate Investment Fund has been supporting climate change resilience with a total financing of approximately US $ 11.5 billion and has the potential to attract an additional $ 63 billion in additional resources, he said. The United States in the form of co-financing to promote development towards effective climate change resilience in the Asia-Pacific region.
During the four days of the "Asia-Pacific Knowledge Exchange Program", participants will learn more about the experiences of countries in the Asia-Pacific region in implementing programs supported by the Climate Investment Fund. Examples are pilot programs for climate resilience, or "PPCR" and forest investment programs, or "FIP".
