All men over the age of 18 in Russia are required to serve in the military for one year or receive equivalent training during higher education.
In July, Russia's lower house of parliament voted to raise the minimum age for men from 27 to 30 years. The new law came into force on January 1, 2024.
Military service has long been a sensitive issue in Russia, with many men trying to escape the two-yearly recall, in the spring and fall.
In September, Putin signed a decree ordering the deployment of 130,000 troops for the autumn campaign. Last spring, Russia also planned to deploy 147,000 troops.
