Dmytro Kuleba arrived in New Delhi on Thursday for a visit aimed at promoting and seeking support for Ukraine's peace plan and strengthening ties with India. Due to its long-standing traditional economic and military ties with Russia, India has refused to criticize or condemn Russia's war against Ukraine, but at the same time has increased its purchases of oil from Russia at a discount.
"We have agreed to restore the level of cooperation between the two countries that existed before Russia began the full-scale war and to set new plans that will take our relationship to a new level," he said.
According to Kuleba, India has benefited greatly from expanding trade and technology relations with Ukraine and will allow Indian companies to contribute to post-war reconstruction.
Ukraine hopes to hold a summit without Russia's participation to push for a peace formula, also known as the withdrawal of Russian forces from its territory. But Moscow has rejected the peace plan, saying any peace summit in Ukraine without a Russian presence would fail.
