(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chin Malin, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Justice, stated that during the 76 days of the campaign to resolve cases in the court system, the Ministry of Justice received 797 complaints, of which 45% complained that there was no Normal and 55% sued to help expedite their case.
Mr. Jean Malin stated this in the press conference on the afternoon of March 29, 2024 on the results of the General Assembly to summarize the work of justice in the country in 2023 and set the priority direction in 2024 for 3 days.
Mr. Malin concluded that of the 797 cases, only 45 percent complained that their case was irregular after the court ruled, and that some cases had not yet been decided by the court. The way of the court because they are worried that the court will make a wrong decision.
According to the senior official of the Ministry of Justice, out of the 45% of the irregularities, (1) the court ruled and he lost the case, and when he lost the case, he complained that the irregularity (2) the court has not decided yet. However, he feared that the court's decision would be unfair and unfair to him. The other 55 percent is a lawsuit urging the Ministry to expedite his case a little faster.
The Ministry of Justice in the seventh term of the Royal Government has launched a judicial reform program. These include the establishment of the National Dispute Resolution Authority and the launch of two campaigns: resolving irregular cases in court and expediting cases in court. The National Dispute Resolution Authority (NACA) began receiving complaints on March 1, 2024.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Justice confirmed this at a press conference on the afternoon of March 29, 2024, on the results of the General Assembly, summarizing the work of the justice sector in 2023 and setting priorities for 2024 for three days.
"We have dealt with this criminal case a lot in the past, we launched a campaign to resolve the criminal congestion in 2020-2021, and we have been very successful, more than 95 percent," said Mr. Malin. .
Russia used its veto power at the UN Security Council on Thursday to block the resumption of a panel of experts tasked with investigating violations of sanctions related to Korea's banned nuclear and ballistic missile programs. North.
