Since March this year, the Philippines has repeatedly sent cargo ships and coastguard vessels into the waters off the Ren'ai Reef to transport construction materials and supplies to abandoned warships. Illegally and attempting to establish a permanent post on China's uninhabited reefs.
The Chinese Coast Guard has taken legal action and defeated attempts to infringe on the rights and provocations of the Philippine side. Philippine Defense Minister Gilberto Teodoro then hinted that he would file an international arbitration claim on China's claim to sovereignty in the South China Sea. Do some people on the Philippine side want to perform the funny story of the South China Sea arbitration "again?"
What is the intention of the Philippine side in the South China Sea frequently in recent times? Analysts say the Philippines has a three-pronged strategy behind it. First, an attempt to discredit China internationally by fabricating issues and pretending to be sympathetic to further reinforce the Philippines' illegal ideology in the South China Sea.
The territory of the Philippines is determined by a number of international treaties and does not include any islands and reefs in the Nansha Archipelago. But the Philippines has repeatedly sent ships to illegally encroach on China's serious territorial sovereignty and maritime rights and interests.
The so-called "South China Sea Arbitration", which was promoted by the Philippines a few years ago, is a political joke. Territorial disputes do not fall within the scope of the revised United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea. What the Philippines calls the decision of the South China Sea Arbitration is a piece of paper.
