BEIJING: China has abolished anti-dumping and anti-subsidy tariffs on Australian wine, marking the end of a three-year campaign to put trade pressure on Canberra and boost hopes for The revival of the multi-billion dollar Australian wine industry. According to the Bangkok Post.
According to the Bangkok Post, the Chinese Ministry of Commerce said in Beijing that the tariffs would take effect on Friday.
"Due to the situation in China's wine market, anti-dumping and anti-subsidy tariffs have been changed," the Ministry of Commerce said in a statement issued on Thursday. "On wine imported from Australia is no longer necessary."
The Australian wine industry has been waiting for the announcement for weeks after some winemakers and lawmakers said China had temporarily recommended lifting tariffs.
The decision to repeal the trade ban has raised hopes of reviving a profitable wine trade between the two countries. However, the chairman of the Australian-Chinese National Business Council warned on Wednesday that winemakers may not fully return to the Chinese market.
