Vietnam calls on US to recognize 'market economy'
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - Vietnam on Tuesday called on the United States to recognize its status as a market economy, saying stronger trade ties would benefit Washington in key areas as Vietnam seeks to diversify. China. According to the AFP news agency.
Vietnam and China are among several countries that the United States identifies as non-market economies, a position that allows for stricter measures on their exports. The government of President Joe Biden in October agreed to review Vietnam's economic status.
"Our friends and US partners have always said that a strong, authoritative, resilient and prosperous Vietnam is in the interests of the United States," Foreign Minister Bui Thanh Son said during a visit to Washington. United States. "We look forward to tighter US action in response to this commitment."
But Bui said Vietnam has given potential to key areas of US interest, including chip production, key minerals and artificial intelligence.
"Our cooperation in this area could enhance Vietnam's role in the regional and global value chain, which benefits the United States and other partners as well," he said. Families in Kampong Thom Province Volunteer to Form a Modern Agricultural Community
