Lok Chumteav Dr. Pech Chanmony Calls for Preservation of Khmer Ceiling While Facing Extreme Loss
(Phnom Penh): The Khmer ceiling, which was once used by Khmer ancestors, is now facing great loss, while the National Museum of Cambodia has only 70 panels.
The hollow ceiling is a hollow that is more special than other hoops because the weaver weaves a Buddhist story and a special lantern. The National Museum of Cambodia has been opening a non-permanent exhibition on the ceiling: the fourth development and challenges.
Attached by Picture of Visiting and Checking the Ceiling Posted on Official Facebook on March 24, 2024 Yesterday, Her Excellency Dr. Pech Chanmony, the exalted wife of His Excellency Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia, called on the public to attend the exhibition. Hold the ceiling at the National Museum and emphasize in the original sense that "together we help preserve our Khmer traditions and culture."
Please be informed that the first Hall Ceiling Exhibition was held in 2010, the second in 2012 and the third in 2016. For this year, the team of the Museum Department has selected 17 hollow ceilings for display from March 21 to June 21, 2024.
The purpose of this exhibition is to share more information about the museum's ceiling weaving collection, to convey the message of the artisans' concern about the risk of loss of traditional Khmer ceiling production, to show the context of changes in Cambodian society on the needs of the ceiling and Current of hollow ceiling production for current use. In addition, this exhibition is also held to celebrate the 104th anniversary of the establishment of the National Museum of Cambodia.
