Before the missile was confirmed to belong to Ukraine, there were fears among NATO members that the possibility of a war could escalate, as Poland is also a member of the military alliance. Genocide and Democracy for Youth, Forced Evacuation under the Khmer Rouge Regime (1975-1979)
(Phnom Penh): From March 23-28, 2024, 68 participants were on a study tour of the crimes of genocide and democracy for young people, forced evictions during the Khmer Rouge regime (1975-1979) on the street Khmer Rouge Railway.
For previous generations who went through World War II in Europe or the Khmer Rouge regime in Cambodia from 1975 to 1979, the railways were a means used for systemic crimes against humanity or genocide through Forced evacuation of many people.
About six million people in Europe and two million in Cambodia are killed. Each station became a place to determine the fate of the people, and all the people died in the camps of the Nazi regime or the killing fields of the Khmer Rouge regime.
These events remind us of the memories of previous generations of how railroads were used for crimes against humanity and genocide at the time. This is a historical event that the next generation will never forget.
As new infrastructure develops, and for decades to come, these historic sites may be lost and survivors of the Khmer Rouge will no longer be able to tell them.
Now may be the best time for young people to visit the historical sites where the genocide took place through the program "Khmer Rouge Railway: A Study Tour on Genocidal Crimes and Democracy for Youth, Forced Displacement In the Khmer Rouge Regime (1975-1979) ”where Cambodian volunteers will meet with survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime to hear the truth.
The study tour has three main objectives, including:
1: Discover the experiences of Khmer Rouge survivors who were forcibly evacuated by train
When the Khmer Rouge came to power, the Khmer Rouge committed a heinous crime, forcibly evicting people from their homes. The evacuation has had physical and psychological consequences and has damaged human dignity.
The Khmer Rouge committed the crime of forcible evacuation in three stages:
* Phase 1: Immediate occupation of cities from April 17 to September 1975. Khmer Rouge soldiers often told the people at gunpoint that they had to leave for only three days to escape the US B-52 bombing.
* Second phase: from after September 1975 to the end of 1977. People from the Central, Southwest, and Eastern Zones were evacuated to Siem Reap, Preah Vihear, and Northwest Zones, including Battambang, Pursat, and Banteay Meanchey provinces. People were told to move to areas with more food and fertile land for cultivation.
2: Increase awareness of the non-compiled / published stories of oral history of survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime
3. Provide opportunities for young people to learn about railways and rail travel to promote democratic participation.
In modern times, trains are used as a means of daily commuting and contribute to efficient and cost-effective social development and avoid environmental impacts that lead to sustainability.
Two important historical perspectives on railways are: 1) the railroad of despair, which has committed genocidal crimes, and 2) the railroad, the hope for the promotion of democracy through the benefits of railways.
