NEW YORK (Reuters) - Gold jumped more than $ 2,200 an ounce for the first time after the US Federal Reserve maintained its vision of three interest rate cuts this year, indicating the bank is not worried about the dollar. Inflation has risen recently.
However, this rapid price increase has surprised many market observers as there is no clear catalyst.
The rise in gold prices was driven in part by expectations of a looser monetary policy in the United States, which was reaffirmed by the US Federal Reserve on Wednesday. US Federal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell said officials would like to see more evidence of steel prices falling, but "it is still likely that many will understand that we will achieve confidence and that interest rates will be cut." "
