Prime Minister Hun Manet considers the promotion of the right to life of persons with disabilities necessary for all persons with disabilities to enjoy the same rights as ordinary citizens (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Considering that the promotion of the right to life of persons with disabilities is necessary to ensure that all persons with disabilities have the same rights as people without disabilities.
On the occasion of inaugurating the achievements of the National Center for the Disabled Techo Sen and the National Paralympic Committee of Cambodia on the morning of March 21, 2024, Samdech Hun Sen highlighted that the rights that people with disabilities have the same rights as ordinary citizens include: The right to full and equal access to education, health care, justice services and participation in social, religious, sports, art, and political activities without discrimination.
The Prime Minister of Cambodia said, "Promoting the right to life of persons with disabilities is essential to ensure that all persons with disabilities have the same rights as people without disabilities, including: "Education, health care, justice services and participation in social, religious, sports, arts, politics fully and fairly without discrimination."
According to Hun Manet, improving and developing the physical environment, public transportation, knowledge, information and communication in the digital age is essential to promoting the sustainable participation of people with disabilities. Discrimination and neglect of people with disabilities is a moral, spiritual and universal misconduct, and leads to a loss of unity, harmony and trust in the community.
At the same time, the President of the Royal Government of Cambodia still affirms that all people with disabilities are not alone, that is, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the Royal Government always pay attention and stick with. Samdech also praised the athletes who have worked hard to honor the motherland by winning medals from various sports events and encourage people with disabilities to continue to adhere to the spirit of "physically disabled, not mentally disabled" to improve their abilities.
