Bank of Japan ends negative interest rate era
TOKYO (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan has scrapped the latest negative interest rate in the world, ending the strongest monetary stimulus program in modern history, while also showing that financial conditions will remain volatile. Depending on the situation.
Bank of Japan Governor Kazuo Ueda told a news conference after the decision to end the negative interest rate: "We have determined that achieving the 2% inflation target is sustainable. "Expectations." "Large-scale monetary easing has served its purpose," he added.
Ueda stressed that despite the negative termination of interest rates, it is important that financial conditions remain flexible. "There is still some gap in achieving 2% inflation if we look at it from the perspective of the expected inflation rate," he said. "Considering this gap, I think we will pursue a normal policy, as I have said before, while maintaining the importance of maintaining a climate-sensitive policy environment," he said.
