The European Union (EU) and the Philippines said earlier this week that they would resume free trade agreement negotiations as the EU seeks to take advantage of Asia's faster economic growth and access to raw materials. Important. According to Reuters.
The EU has set a goal of negotiating agreements with Southeast Asian countries, reaching agreements with Singapore and Vietnam, and is negotiating with Indonesia and Thailand. The EU is looking at Philippine raw materials such as nickel, copper and chromite, which it needs for the transition to environmentally friendly industries, and for now Europe is heavily dependent on China.
The Philippines has already benefited from the EU's GSP + preferential treatment system for developing countries, but the Southeast Asian nation intends to upgrade to a higher middle-income country where the Philippines will no longer receive GSP +. No more.
The Philippines currently benefits from entering the EU market duty-free for about two-thirds of its products, including palm oil, vacuum cleaners, tuna and pineapple. Pascual said the free trade agreement could allow the export of seaweed, tobacco, timber and ornamental plants. According to Reuters.
The move is aimed at reducing smog, which the Thai government has strongly blamed on neighboring countries. According to the Bangkok Post.
Thai government spokesman Chai Wacharonke was quoted by the Bangkok Post as saying that the Thai cabinet had instructed the Ministry of Commerce to impose a ban on the import of corn from areas that practice burning agricultural waste on agricultural land to combat smog. Which occurs annually, affecting health.
He said the decision was an expression of the Thai government's concern over pollution caused by the burning of agricultural waste in neighboring countries.
To confirm the involvement of the incineration card, Chai said, officials who made the decision would compare the corn growing area with a satellite imagery showing the incineration.
