Putin's landslide victory raises concerns in Ukraine
Russia's landslide victory in the election has brought joy to some countries, but bad news for others trying to block Russian influence. While the United States, the West and Ukraine have condemned the election process in Russia, the turnout was noted to be higher than in 2018.
According to the results of the election, 71-year-old Vladimir Putin is set to run for another six-year term, making him Russia's longest-serving leader in more than 200 years. This of Russian history.
Putin hailed the first results as "showing the" confidence "and" hope "of the Russian people in his image to lead Russia in what he has planned for the future. Russia is one of the few countries in the world that opposes the current principles of global order under the leadership of the United States and its Western allies.
"I would like to thank all the Russians that we are united in going to the polls, or that the Russian people are the main source of Russian power," Putin said.
In his speech, Putin expressed his interest in developing a strategy for war on Ukrainian territory, describing border demarcation and that he would have to work on more important issues in particular. Involved in the war in Ukraine:
"In general, the initiative is entirely on the side of the Russian armed forces in some directions, where our troops have defeated the enemy or our forces are advancing gradually on the battlefield every day," Putin said. "Russia is more active than the defense we see every day."
He vowed to further strengthen the military and defense capabilities of his role as Russia's leader in the future.
For Western leaders, the inevitable landslide victory of Vladimir Putin in this election is a message that Russia's policy towards Ukraine will continue in the same resolutions and expectations. Defeating Russia under Putin is unlikely to achieve Western aspirations for Ukraine.
After the Russian election, Putin criticized the US justice system and politics, saying the world had ridiculed US policy, but said he would work with it. Any American leader who will win the election in November 2024.
The volatility of US domestic politics has become a concern of some countries that were once key to Putin's opposition to war and were determined not to let Putin win the war.
The French president has voiced his desire to send more NATO troops to Ukraine, which has been a warning from Putin that a nuclear war could erupt.
Putin has warned the West that the presence of Western troops on Ukrainian territory could push the world to the brink of World War III, but Putin has hinted that he is ready to discuss a proposal. Of France for a ceasefire in Ukrainian territory.
"Even though I do not know the statement of the French president, we will be ready to discuss all the issues, but as we have always said, every situation we are going to start with," said Vladimir Putin. "Which is in the interests of the Russian Federation, especially the current situation on the battlefield."
Amid reports of Putin's landslide victory, Ukraine has reportedly fired several drones at Russian territory. Russia's Defense Ministry says 35 drones fired by Ukraine at targets on Russian territory in the Moscow area have caused fires at refineries, disrupting power supplies along the border. But did not cause any casualties.
So far, Putin has never acknowledged that he launched a war of aggression in Ukraine, calling it a special military operation. Putin described the election, which was attended by 74 percent of the 112 million registered voters, as a sign that Russia was being forced to take up arms to protect its national interests.
3. The war in Ukraine will continue to be a central issue for the Kremlin in a speech to the Russian people in February, which is considered a key campaign speech, Putin promised that Russia will continue to achieve its goals in Ukraine while Investing in Russia's economy, infrastructure and long-term goal is to boost the country's birth rate.
4. Russians wait to see what happens: Concerns have often been raised after the Russian presidential election, when the Kremlin routinely introduced unpopular policies. After the 2018 presidential election, for example, Putin raised his retirement age. Russians are now waiting to see if the Kremlin will announce more troops or increase domestic restrictions.
