Sri Lanka's economy is expected to shrink 2.3 percent by 2023 as the island nation struggles to get out of its worst financial crisis in a year, official data showed on Friday. Decades. According to Reuters.
Reuters reported, citing a press release issued by Sri Lanka's Census and Statistics Department, that despite the annual economic slowdown, the economy grew 4.5 percent in the fourth quarter, indicating that The economy is starting to recover this year.
Sri Lanka's agricultural sector grew 2.6 percent from 2022, but industrial output fell 9.2 percent while the services sector fell 0.2 percent.
"Economic growth has benefited from a rise in the value of the currency, increased remittances and improved tourism revenue in the second half of 2023, which has boosted positive sentiment," the statement added. Economy.
Sri Lanka's economy is expected to shrink by 7.8 percent in 2022 due to the severe foreign exchange crisis, coupled with political instability that has led to a drop in the value of its currency, rising inflation and rising interest rates.
With $ 2.9 billion in International Monetary Fund assistance received in March last year, Sri Lanka's economy is on the verge of recovery and is expected to grow 1.8 percent. This year.
