His Excellency Hun Manet stressed to the Council of Ministers that the biggest task of the Royal Government is to serve the people to ensure the safety and improve the lives of the people. South Korean investment inflows to China hit record low
SEOUL: South Korea's direct investment inflows to China last year fell the most in more than three decades, signaling a slump in economic ties between the two countries as the United States pushed its allies. Its to reduce dependence on Beijing. According to Bloomberg News.
The figures reflect the shift in China's importance to South Korea's economy, which relies heavily on foreign demand and production, as Washington seeks to weaken Beijing's influence on the global supply chain.
The drop in figures also underscores foreign business pessimism in China, with foreign direct investment in the world's second-largest economy last year at a record low. Since the early 1990s.
Also last year, Japanese companies added the smallest investment amount in at least a decade. Taiwanese companies are also increasingly reluctant to increase their investment in China, with new investments last year being the lowest since 2001.
One of the drivers that led to a slowdown in South Korean investment flows to China was the collapse of Korean car sales there. Geopolitical factors, intense price wars and the rapid transition to electric cars also contributed to the decline. According to Bloomberg News.
