Russia is holding a presidential election with the firm belief that Vladimir Putin can continue his rule for another decade. If Putin wins the election and retains the presidency of Russia for the next six years, Ukraine will face more war pressure in 2024 due to a lack of weapons and ammunition.
Russia's official election for a new president began on March 15 for three days, although some elections were held ahead of schedule, including in eastern Ukraine, which Russia has cut. Used as a satellite area in September 2023.
The Kiev government has claimed that the elections in the Donetsk, Luhansk, Zaporizia, Kherson and Crimea, which are now under Russian control, were illegal and invalid. As the territories occupied by Russia have already been made with previous indications by opinion polls that people still support Vladimir Putin to continue as Russian leader.
The polls show that there is no influence that could cause Putin to lose control of Russia in the new term. Vladimir Putin has called on Russians to go to the polls, saying the results will be "very useful" and will affect Russia's development.
"I would like to emphasize that the only source of power in our country is the Russian people," Vladimir Putin told Russians. "This is an important point in the constitution, which means that only you, the Russian citizen, will determine the fate of our homeland," he said.
**** Russia and the new presidential election
The election results are expected to be announced soon, with the winner set to be inaugurated in May for a six-year term, with no one expected to run for office. Vladimir Putin. The second round of voting will take place three weeks after this weekend, if no candidate receives more than half of the votes cast. The election was also conducted online remotely.
