(Phnom Penh): EU Ambassador to Cambodia Igor Driesmans and Minister of Mines and Energy Keo Rattanak jointly inaugurate eight new national radiation monitoring stations across the capital and provinces bordering Cambodia to boost national capacity. For a timely response to the risks of radiation in the atmosphere.
The handover ceremony of the new National Radiation Level Monitoring Station was held on the afternoon of March 14, 2024 at the Ministry of Mines and Energy.
These National Radiation Level Monitoring Stations will boost the national capacity for timely response to airborne radiation risks in terms of public health and safety and environmental protection. This is part of the EU-ASEAN Partnership to Strengthen Regional Cooperation on Radiation and Nuclear Emergency Preparedness and Response (EP&R) in ASEAN in line with international best practices.
Minister of Mines and Energy Keo Rattanak said the launch of the Gamma Dose radiation monitoring station was a breakthrough designed to combine resources and skills to address common challenges.
He acknowledged that nuclear material and technology, including iodine radiation, provided significant benefits but posed significant risks. These technologies have the potential to benefit humanity in many ways, from medical applications to energy production. It also brings with it the challenges of preventing accidents and emergencies.
"Even though Cambodia does not have nuclear power plants, the establishment of these stations in Cambodia promotes our commitment to nuclear safety, environmental protection and protecting our people from the harmful effects of radiation in the event of a nuclear accident," he added. "Or a radiation emergency occurs in the area."
"With the increasing use of nuclear technology and the potential for radiation exposure, we must have the necessary infrastructure to monitor and evaluate radiation levels in our area," he added. "The facilities we launch today empower us with real-time data that allows for active decision-making to protect public health and the environment."
Through this ASEAN partnership and close coordination with the ASEAN Atomic Energy Regulatory Network (ASEANTOM), Cambodia has installed eight systems at environmental radiation monitoring stations in Phnom Penh, Ratanakkiri, Oddar Meanchey, Koh Kong, Sihanoukville, Kampot and Kep. Backup system for replacement.
Speaking at the event, EU Ambassador to Cambodia Igor Driesmans stressed the importance of emergency preparedness, including in the context of environmental radiation monitoring to protect communities and ecosystems.
