Russia begins presidential election as Ukraine steps up attacks on border areas
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russia's presidential election has begun on Friday morning, with elections expected to give President Vladimir Putin another six-year term. The vote was meaningless and escalated attacks on the Russian border. This is according to a release by the AFP news agency.
Officials in Moscow have warned against protests during the March 15-17 presidential election, following calls for protests against Putin on Sunday. Opposition. The Kremlin has said the election will show the support of the Russian people for the war in Ukraine and that elections will be held in Ukrainian territory controlled by the Russian military.
In the run-up to the election, Kiev has stepped up attacks on the Russian border, while Russian National Guard forces have been fighting pro-Ukrainian forces in the Kursk region in the latest clashes on the border.
