(Kiev): The Ukrainian leader says the situation on the battlefield has improved, with Ukrainian forces able to withstand Russian aggression after a series of territorial losses when the key city of Adivka was captured. Russia came to power. But the situation for Ukraine in 2024 is still not clear.
The NATO Secretary-General has reiterated that the North Atlantic Treaty Organization has no plans to send allied troops to Ukraine. The statement from NATO leader Jens Stoltenberg came as Moscow government officials said they had information about a NATO adviser visiting Ukraine.
The news about NATO is now considered sensitive following the remarks by French leader Emmanuel Macron, who wants to urge the deployment of NATO infantry to fight in the war. Territories of Ukraine in 2024.
The remarks by French leader Emmanuel Macron in late February led to an immediate warning from Russian leaders of the consequences of a nuclear war if NATO troops were deployed in Ukraine. Yes, that.
For the North Atlantic military alliance, the idea of sending troops to Ukraine has been banned, especially as NATO seeks to avoid being dragged into a larger war with nuclear-armed Russia. But nothing prevents NATO members from participating in such activities individually or as a group, and NATO itself can only participate if all 32 of its members agree.
Britain, Germany, Sweden, the Czech Republic, Italy and Hungary were among the countries that said they were not considering sending infantry to Ukraine after Macron's controversial remarks in late February.
"We have not yet fully agreed on the deployment of infantry to Ukraine, but in that sense nothing will be denied," McCormack said in February. "We will do everything we can to ensure that Russia will not win the war," he said.
The Polish foreign minister's remarks also reflected a major debate between European member states in the past over how they could help Ukraine at a time when Russia has the upper hand on the battlefield. Weakness on ammunition.
The bold statements of France and Poland, who supported the idea of sending NATO infantry to help Ukraine, were strongly opposed by Italy. Italy has criticized France and Poland over the deployment. Italian Defense Minister Guido Crosetto has stated that France and Poland have no right to speak on behalf of all NATO member states.
How would the Ukraine War of 2024 develop without negotiations?
European countries are under pressure to think about the future of Ukraine's war at a time when the United States is embroiled in internal tensions that have led to a blockage of 61 billion military aid to Ukraine and a busy schedule. As for the November 2024 general election, the biggest concern is that Ukraine will be lost if Donald Trump returns to power in the White House.
In February, Donald Trump expressed his displeasure with NATO, which has always made the United States the largest country in charge of spending the blood of Americans to defend 30 countries. Past member. "The United States will not provide protection to NATO allies who do not spend enough on defense," Donald Trump said.
Donald Trump has even said he will "encourage" Russia to attack NATO members who do not meet the Alliance's goal of spending 2% of its annual GDP on military service. . Donald Trump's warning has been seen as prompting some NATO countries to help target 2% of annual GDP spending on defense.
According to NATO, countries seeking to join the North Atlantic Treaty Organization are obliged to spend 2 percent of their national GDP on the military until 2024, but reports suggest that there are still some countries that. The cost of this desired size has not been disclosed as scheduled.
