India, EFTA sign $ 100 billion free trade agreement
New Delhi: India and the four-member European Free Trade Association (EFTA) signed a $ 100 billion free trade agreement on Sunday to boost investment and exports. According to the AFP news agency.
He added that the agreement "will pave the way for mutual growth and prosperity" by boosting exports, promoting investment and creating jobs.
The agreement was signed after 16 years of negotiations. According to the AFP news agency, quoting Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi in a statement read at the signing ceremony, the trade agreement "represents our joint commitment to "Open trade, justice and equity."
The EFTA was established in 1960 to promote free trade and economic integration among its members. By 2021, this trade association blog will be the 10th largest in the world in terms of goods and the eighth largest in terms of services. According to the AFP news agency.
