Understand the history of International Women's Day, March 8, since when is it celebrated? For what?
In 2024, the 113th anniversary of International Women's Day, Cambodia, as well as countries around the world, organized this day under the theme "Women and girls in the digital revolution".
1909 - According to a statement from the American Socialist Party, the first National Women's Day is celebrated throughout the United States on February 28. The women continued to commemorate this anniversary until the last Sunday of that month in 1913.
In addition to the right to vote and the right to serve the state, these women demanded the right to work, the right to receive training, and the right to end discrimination in employment. Nearly a week later, on March 25, a tragic fire at the New York City Triangle Blast Triangle Factory claimed the lives of more than 140 factory workers, most of them immigrants. Italian and Jewish.
The event had a profound impact on labor law in the United States, and the disaster-stricken working conditions were commemorated during a series of ceremonies to mark International Women's Day.
* 1913-1914 As part of the pre-World War I peace movement, Russian women celebrated their first International Women's Day on the last Sunday in February 1913. . Elsewhere in Europe, on or about March 8 of the following year, women gathered to protest against the war or to show solidarity with their sisters.
* 1917 - With 2 million Russian soldiers, Russian women once again choose the last Sunday in February to strike for "food and peace." Political leaders have opposed the timing of the strike, but the women are still moving forward.
International Women's Day is becoming more and more a time to reflect on the progress made, to call for change and to celebrate the acts of courage and commitment of ordinary women who have played an extraordinary role in History of women's rights.
