Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth chaired a meeting of the Steering Committee Meeting of the One-Stop-Shop Project and urged the participating ministries and institutions to work hard to implement it successfully.
The "One Nation Standing System" deals with the issuance of licenses, certificates, permits and other documents in electronic form for export-import goods that are on the list of prohibited goods and restricted goods, as well as exempted goods. Taxes and duties in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
"National Single Standing Project Phase 3" has expanded to 11 more ministries and institutions. At the same time, the "one-stop-shop system" also added four new functions, including risk management function, business document binding function, authorized economic operator function and single-user account management function.
Based on the progress achieved above and through the discussion of the meeting, the General Doctor highly appreciated the ministries-institutions that participate in the implementation of the "one-stop-shop system" fully and proposed to the ministries and institutions that are not fully or not yet. Participated in the implementation of efforts and provide proactive cooperation to implement the third phase of the National Single Project project successfully in accordance with the set time.
(Ratanakkiri): Farmers in Ratanakkiri province have started harvesting cashew nuts from 25-30 percent as yields have declined and prices have fallen from last year. They are worried about losses.
Oeun Ny, a plantation owner in Vern Sai district, said that this year's cashew plantation harvest was very low and the loss was that the cashew flowers withered.
He said that his cashew plantation is about 20 hectares, and the cost of labor and other medicines cost a lot, but the yield is less than in previous years.
The farmer confirms that the yield is declining and the traders are shaking the price optionally, that is, falling every day. If it rains for a month, his family and other cashew farmers fall in price and the losses are high.
Prosecutors indict two Koreans after arrest at Phnom Penh International Airport with more than 2 kg of drugs
(Phnom Penh): Representatives of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court have decided to charge two Korean couples after their arrest at Phnom Penh International Airport with more than 2 kilograms of drugs on March 4, 2024.
According to a press release issued by Phnom Penh Municipal Court on March 7, 2024, on March 4, 2024, anti-drug police officers cooperated with the authorities. Customs and Excise at Phnom Penh International Airport cracked down on drug cases at the departure point of Phnom Penh International Airport.
As a result, the police arrested two Korean suspects and found 1,290.29 grams of methamphetamine and 987.42 grams of ketamine. After completing this operation, the police prepared a case file and sent the suspect to the Phnom Penh Municipal Court. As for the factions involved in this drug trafficking case, the authorities have been investigating and sending it to the Phnom Penh Municipal Court for legal action.
